<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  Application manifest for Cliche.

  THIS FILE MUST STAY PURE ASCII. DO NOT WRITE "Cliche" WITH ITS ACCENT HERE.
  Measured on 2 September 2026, and it cost a debugging session:
    - this source file held "Cliche" with an e-acute, correctly encoded UTF-8
      (bytes C3 A9);
    - tauri-build routes the manifest through a generated .rc file, and the
      resource compiler re-encodes it to the ANSI code page. The embedded
      manifest then held a lone E9 byte;
    - a lone E9 is not valid UTF-8, while the declaration above claims UTF-8.
      The Windows SxS loader rejects the whole manifest.
  The failure mode is nasty: `cargo build` SUCCEEDS. The binary is produced and
  then refuses to start with os error 14001, "side-by-side configuration is
  incorrect", reported at line 1. Nothing points at this file.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
  Tauri 2 does embed a default manifest, but that default declares ONLY the
  Common-Controls v6 dependency below - it says nothing about DPI. Verified on
  2 September 2026 by reading
  crates/tauri-build/src/windows-app-manifest.xml on the tauri-apps/tauri
  `dev` branch and on the tauri-build-v2.5.0 tag: both contain the dependency
  block and nothing else. So per-monitor DPI awareness has to be declared here.

  WHY THE MANIFEST AND NOT THE RUNTIME CALL
  tao (Tauri's windowing layer) has `become_dpi_aware()`, which calls
  SetProcessDpiAwarenessContext(PER_MONITOR_AWARE_V2). Microsoft nevertheless
  documents the manifest as the recommended route: the API call happens after
  process start, so any DPI-dependent call made before it wins, and the call
  then fails with ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED. The manifest is applied before a single
  line of our code runs. The two are consistent, not in conflict - the manifest
  simply gets there first.

  WHY THE COMMON-CONTROLS BLOCK IS COPIED HERE
  WindowsAttributes::app_manifest() REPLACES the default manifest wholesale, it
  does not merge. Deleting the dependency block below would silently drop
  visual styles and send every common control back to the Windows 95 look.
-->
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <dependency>
    <dependentAssembly>
      <assemblyIdentity
        type="win32"
        name="Microsoft.Windows.Common-Controls"
        version="6.0.0.0"
        processorArchitecture="*"
        publicKeyToken="6595b64144ccf1df"
        language="*"
      />
    </dependentAssembly>
  </dependency>

  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <!-- Windows 10 and Windows 11 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" />
      <!-- Windows 8.1 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{1f676c76-80e1-4239-95bb-83d0f6d0da78}" />
    </application>
  </compatibility>

  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <!--
        Windows 10 1607 and later read this one. PerMonitorV2 is the only mode
        that rescales non-client areas (title bar, menus, scrollbars) and that
        delivers WM_DPICHANGED, which is what a screenshot tool needs to keep
        its geometry honest when a window moves between screens.
      -->
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
      <!--
        Fallback for Windows 8.1 through Windows 10 1507, which ignore the tag
        above. "true/pm" means: system-aware on Vista..8.0, per-monitor on 8.1+.
      -->
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true/pm</dpiAware>
      <!--
        Screenshots get saved under user-chosen paths that can exceed MAX_PATH.
      -->
      <longPathAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">true</longPathAware>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
